  "text": "إدارية step by step:\n1. The problem states that the tetrahedron's four vertices have integer coordinates, and it has four different altitudes of which three measure \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) units each.\n2. The question asks for the maximum possible length of the fourth altitude.\n3. To maximize the length of the fourth altitude, we should minimize the lengths of the other altitudes, which are already given as \\(\\sqrt{80}\\).\n4. Since the vertices have integer coordinates, the altitude from a vertex to the opposite edge will form a right triangle with the edge as one leg and the altitude as the other leg.\n5. The area of the tetrahedron can be expressed using the altitudes and the corresponding edge lengths in a similar manner to the area of a triangle.\n6. The area of the tetrahedron can be distributed among the three altitudes \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) and the fourth altitude, but the total area must be conserved.\n\nLet's denote the three altitudes as \\(a = b = c = \\sqrt{80}\\) and the fourth altitude as \\(d\\). The total area \\(A\\) of the tetrahedron can be expressed as:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{edge length} \\times \\text{altitude} \\]\nThus, the total area can be expressed in terms of the three altitudes and any corresponding edge lengths:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} a \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } a + \\frac{1}{2} b \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } b + \\frac{1}{2} c \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } c + \\frac{1}{2} d \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } d \\]\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{80} \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } a + \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{80} \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } b + \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{80} \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } c + \\frac{1}{2} d \\times \\text{edge length for altitude } d \\]\n\nSince \\(a = b = c = \\sqrt{80}\\), we can simplify the equation:\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{80} \\left( \\text{edge length for altitude } a + \\text{edge length for altitude } b